# Workflow safety

A workflow multiplies whatever it does by the number of agents it spawns. That
applies to its mistakes as much as its work.

## Confirm before launching

Never start a real run without showing the user the phase plan, the projected
agent count, and which phases can write. Wait for them to agree.

Cursor has no per-run approval card, so this confirmation and the launch-gate
hook are the only things between a mistaken argument and hundreds of agents. A
dry run (`--dry-run`) needs no confirmation — it spawns nothing.

## Never leave a run invisible

A background run the user cannot see is a black box, and they should not have to
ask for a way in. Whenever you launch one, in that same message: paste the
"How to watch this run" block that `wf tmux` prints, and open the dashboard URL
with the `cursor-app-control` tool `open_resource`.

Do not run `tmux attach` from your own shell. The agent shell is `TERM=dumb` and
tmux refuses to attach to a non-TTY; the attach command is for the user's
terminal. `tmux capture-pane -pt <session>` is the one that works for you.

## Read-only by default

Mark every phase `readOnly: true` unless it genuinely must write. Discovery,
audit, review, verification, research, and synthesis never need to write.

When a phase does write, keep it to one clearly identified phase, and give
concurrent writers separate working directories (git worktrees). Agents sharing
a working copy will overwrite each other's edits, and the failure is silent.

Collecting those worktrees back into the main tree is its own trap: a mishandled
patch strands every writer's work, also silently. Follow the capture-and-apply
rules in `skills/authoring-workflows/SKILL.md` instead of improvising them, and
never remove a worktree whose patch has not applied.

## Terminate

Every loop needs both a round cap and a stall detector: stop when consecutive
rounds stop making progress, not just when the round budget runs out. Leave the
runtime's agent cap in place; it exists to bound a runaway script.

## Spend

- `CURSOR_API_KEY` usage is billed separately from the IDE session. Say so
  before the first run rather than after.
- Dry-run every new script. It's free and catches most errors.
- Run against one directory before the whole repo.
- Route cheap phases to a smaller model instead of running everything on the
  session default.
- Stopping is cheap: completed agents stay cached, and resume re-runs only
  unfinished work. Recommend stopping when a run is spending more than expected.

## Report honestly

- An agent that could not verify a claim has produced an unknown, not a
  refutation. Report unverified claims separately; never fold them into the
  confirmed findings and never silently drop them.
- Report failed agents in the totals. A run that says "12 findings" while eight
  agents errored is misleading.
- Never present a workflow's output as more certain than its verification pass
  supports.
